  "summary": "Monolithic churches carved directly into rock cliffs and mountains in northern Ethiopia.",
  "description": "Characterized by intricate facades, internal spaces carved from single rock formations, and elaborate architectural details. Created during the Zagwe dynasty as symbolic recreations of Jerusalem and centers of religious devotion.",
  "culturalContext": "Ethiopia's rock-hewn churches — Lalibela's eleven (12th-13th c., UNESCO), carved down into living rock as a \"New Jerusalem\", plus Tigray's cliff churches (Debre Damo, Abuna Yemata reachable only by cliff climb) — are monolithic sacred architecture unmatched anywhere: Bete Giyorgis cut as a Greek cross pit-church, columns, windows and drainage carved in one act of subtraction. Priests still serve;pilgrims fill the trenches at Genna and Timkat. The architecture proves Ethiopian Christianity's 1,600-year continuity.",
